  "description_draft": "AI collapsed the production side of design work \u2014 layouts, variants, wireframes, working sites \u2014 down to one person and about $150 a month in tools. This episode breaks down the solo subscription design agency model: the most-cited case (Designjoy, reportedly $1M\u2013$3.1M/yr depending on the source), the venture-scale proof of market (Superside, $44.9M ARR with 850 employees), and the honest gap in between. We name the number variance instead of picking the biggest one, show the real tool stack, and walk through what actually gets a first client in this category. Includes the build plan, realistic economics by effort level, and the failure modes nobody puts on the pricing page.\n\nSources and figures cited on screen \u2014 full list in the pinned comment.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "hook",
      "beats": [
        {
          "beat": 1,
          "vo_text": "One person, no employees, reportedly pulling in somewhere between one million and three million dollars a year \u2014 from a design agency that costs about ninety-five dollars a month to run. A venture-backed competitor sells the exact same thing with eight hundred and fifty employees. This is the gap between the two.",
          "highlight_words": [
            "one person",
            "three million",
            "ninety-five dollars",
            "eight hundred and fifty"
          ],
          "asset_hint": "slide: '1 person vs 850 employees \u2014 same product' with two numbers large",
          "source": "getLatka \u2014 Superside headcount; Designjoy self-reported range"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "thesis",
      "beats": [
        {
          "beat": 1,
          "vo_text": "A design subscription agency sells ongoing creative work \u2014 logos, decks, landing pages, working sites \u2014 for a flat monthly fee instead of billing by the project. Startups and small companies use it because it's cheaper than hiring a designer and faster than posting a job.",
          "highlight_words": [
            "flat monthly fee",
            "cheaper than hiring"
          ],
          "asset_hint": "slide: 'Subscription design: flat fee, unlimited requests, no employee'",
          "source": "Brainy Papers \u2014 productized design services model [vendor]"
        },
        {
          "beat": 2,
          "vo_text": "The reason one person can now do this is Figma. Its AI features hit seventy percent adoption among active users this year \u2014 the same layout and variant generation that used to take a team now runs inside a single file, operated by one person.",
          "highlight_words": [
            "seventy percent",
            "one person"
          ],
          "asset_hint": "screen_rec: Figma Make generating layout variants",
          "source": "Figma Q1 2026 disclosure \u2014 70% AI adoption [reported]"
        },
        {
          "beat": 3,
          "vo_text": "Built solo and part-time, this looks like a handful of retainer clients on the side. Built as a full-time operation, the reference case in this category runs roughly thirty-five concurrent clients on one flat price. Both versions run on the same stack \u2014 what changes is hours, not tools.",
          "highlight_words": [
            "part-time",
            "thirty-five concurrent clients"
          ],
          "asset_hint": "slide: 'Part-time: 3-5 clients | Full-time: 30+ clients \u2014 same tools'",
          "source": "Medium/Zack Liu \u2014 Designjoy operating shape [reported, self-sourced]"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "evidence",
      "beats": [
        {
          "beat": 1,
          "vo_text": "The reference case here is Designjoy, run by one operator, Brett Williams, since 2017. And the numbers around it are genuinely messy \u2014 worth saying plainly instead of picking the flattering one.",
          "highlight_words": [
            "one operator",
            "messy"
          ],
          "asset_hint": "logo: Designjoy",
          "source": "Starter Story, Startup Stash, startupfounderstories \u2014 Designjoy figures"
        },
        {
          "beat": 2,
          "vo_text": "Depending on which post or which secondary blog you read, Designjoy has been reported at one million a year, one-point-five million, one-point-seven million ARR, two million-plus, and three-point-one million in 2024. Every one of those traces back to Brett's own posts. There is no audited figure.",
          "highlight_words": [
            "one million",
            "three-point-one million",
            "no audited figure"
          ],
          "asset_hint": "chart: Designjoy reported revenue across 5 sources, same year range, wildly different bars",
          "source": "Aggregated: Starter Story, Startup Stash, startupfounderstories, secondary blogs [reported, self-sourced \u26a0\ufe0f]"
        },
        {
          "beat": 3,
          "vo_text": "What's consistent across every version of the story: zero contractors, zero employees, around thirty-five clients at a time, flat forty-nine ninety-five a month, no client meetings, about five hours of work a day. He started on a twenty-nine dollar website template and a Product Hunt launch.",
          "highlight_words": [
            "zero employees",
            "forty-nine ninety-five",
            "twenty-nine dollar"
          ],
          "asset_hint": "slide: 'Designjoy operating shape: 0 employees \u00b7 35 clients \u00b7 $4,995/mo \u00b7 5 hrs/day'",
          "source": "Medium/Zack Liu; startupfounderstories [reported \u26a0\ufe0f]"
        },
        {
          "beat": 4,
          "vo_text": "My own version of this, BusyLobby, is not a revenue case yet \u2014 I want to be direct about that. It's a working pipeline: scout hotel accounts, score them against a rubric, build the demo before I ever pitch, send outreach, track who opens the demo, then get on a call. Six outreach emails, one callback, first close in progress.",
          "highlight_words": [
            "not a revenue case",
            "demo before I ever pitch",
            "six outreach",
            "one callback"
          ],
          "asset_hint": "screen_rec: BusyLobby pipeline CSV and PostHog demo analytics dashboard",
          "source": "First-party \u2014 busylobby repo, tracking/autopilot-pipeline.csv, PostHog analytics"
        },
        {
          "beat": 5,
          "vo_text": "[POV: add one specific detail from building a BusyLobby demo \u2014 what the demo-first approach actually looked like for one real prospect]",
          "highlight_words": [
            "demo-first"
          ],
          "asset_hint": "screen_rec: one demo build shown live, before/after",
          "source": null
        },
        {
          "beat": 6,
          "vo_text": "Now the other end of this. Superside sells the identical shape of business \u2014 design as a subscription \u2014 and did forty-four-point-nine million in ARR in 2024, up from thirty-point-eight million the year before. They've raised thirty-five million and employ roughly eight hundred and fifty people across four hundred and fifty clients.",
          "highlight_words": [
            "forty-four-point-nine million",
            "eight hundred and fifty people"
          ],
          "asset_hint": "logo: Superside; chart: Superside ARR 2023 vs 2024",
          "source": "getLatka; PitchBook \u2014 Superside ARR and headcount [reported]"
        },
        {
          "beat": 7,
          "vo_text": "Their minimum is fifteen thousand dollars a month on an annual contract. Designjoy's list price is five thousand. Same category, same subscription model \u2014 roughly three orders of magnitude apart in headcount to deliver it. That gap is either an efficiency story or a warning about what one person can't yet cover.",
          "highlight_words": [
            "fifteen thousand dollars",
            "three orders of magnitude"
          ],
          "asset_hint": "slide: 'Superside: 850 people, $15K min | Designjoy: 1 person, $5K'",
          "source": "Vendr \u2014 Superside pricing [reported]"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "stack",
      "beats": [
        {
          "beat": 1,
          "vo_text": "The stack that shows up again and again in this category is two or three tools deep, not seven. Plan in Figma or Relume, produce in Figma Make, v0, or Lovable, ship in Framer, and generate imagery in Midjourney.",
          "highlight_words": [
            "two or three tools"
          ],
          "asset_hint": "slide: 'Plan \u2192 Produce \u2192 Ship \u2192 Image: 4 tools, not 7'",
          "source": "Guideflow \u2014 AI design tool stacking patterns [vendor]"
        },
        {
          "beat": 2,
          "vo_text": "Figma with Figma Make is the design surface \u2014 AI generation happens inside the same file, and it's free to start. Relume handles wireframing and sitemaps at thirty-eight dollars a month solo. v0 and Lovable turn a prompt into working code or a full site, running free to about twenty-five dollars a month at the tier a solo operator needs.",
          "highlight_words": [
            "free to start",
            "thirty-eight dollars",
            "twenty-five dollars"
          ],
          "asset_hint": "screen_rec: v0 generating a component from a text prompt",
          "source": "buildmvpfast \u2014 Framer/v0/Lovable pricing comparison [vendor]"
        },
        {
          "beat": 3,
          "vo_text": "Framer ships the final site for twenty to forty dollars a month. Midjourney covers hero imagery for ten to sixty. Claude handles copy and client-facing writing for twenty to a hundred. Designjoy's own reported tool cost is ninety-five dollars a month total \u2014 the client pays for their own software, not you.",
          "highlight_words": [
            "ninety-five dollars",
            "client pays for their own"
          ],
          "asset_hint": "slide: 'Total stack: under $200/mo. Designjoy reports $95/mo.'",
          "source": "startupfounderstories \u2014 Designjoy tool cost [reported \u26a0\ufe0f]"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "playbook",
      "beats": [
        {
          "beat": 1,
          "vo_text": "Week one: pick a narrow niche \u2014 landing pages for SaaS, or hotel websites, or pitch decks \u2014 and build three demo pieces for real companies who haven't asked for them. No client yet. The demo is the pitch.",
          "highlight_words": [
            "demo is the pitch"
          ],
          "asset_hint": "slide: 'Week 1: 3 demos, 0 clients'",
          "source": null
        },
        {
          "beat": 2,
          "vo_text": "The scout-and-score approach I use on BusyLobby applies directly here: build a short list of target companies, score each one on how badly their current site or deck is underperforming, then build the fix before you ever email them.",
          "highlight_words": [
            "scout-and-score",
            "build the fix before"
          ],
          "asset_hint": "screen_rec: scoring spreadsheet with rubric columns",
          "source": "First-party \u2014 busylobby pipeline methodology"
        },
        {
          "beat": 3,
          "vo_text": "Week two through four: send that finished demo cold, not a pitch deck about your services. Track who opens it. A tool like PostHog shows you exactly which prospects looked twice \u2014 those are your first calls.",
          "highlight_words": [
            "finished demo cold",
            "who opens it"
          ],
          "asset_hint": "screen_rec: PostHog demo-view analytics",
          "source": "First-party \u2014 busylobby PostHog analytics"
        },
        {
          "beat": 4,
          "vo_text": "Price the first client under market \u2014 somewhere in the twenty-five hundred range \u2014 to get a working case study fast, then move toward the four to seven thousand band once you have one finished project to show.",
          "highlight_words": [
            "twenty-five hundred",
            "four to seven thousand"
          ],
          "asset_hint": "slide: 'Client 1: $2,500 (case study) \u2192 Client 4+: $4,500-$7,000'",
          "source": "Brainy Papers \u2014 $2,500-$7,500 solo pricing band [vendor]"
        },
        {
          "beat": 5,
          "vo_text": "Month two onward is capacity math: at flat monthly pricing, adding client six is easier than getting client one, because the stack is already built. The harder question is where the ceiling sits before one person can't keep the turnaround promise.",
          "highlight_words": [
            "capacity math",
            "ceiling"
          ],
          "asset_hint": "chart: hours per week vs number of concurrent clients, showing diminishing turnaround",
          "source": null
        }
      ]
